  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests that Phyllofolactones F may interact with HUVEC through a series of molecular pathways involving T-cell surface glycoprotein CD3 and other compounds. Specifically, Phyllofolactones F is identified as a target of 2-(1a,4-dimethyl-3,3a,4,5,6,7,7a,7b-octahydro-2H-naphtho[1,2-b]oxiren-7-yl)prop-2-enoic acid, which in turn targets HUVEC. Similarly, Phyllofolactones F is also linked to Lup-20(29)-en-3-ol, a compound that also targets HUVEC. These findings indicate that Phyllofolactones F may exert its biological effects through the activation of pathways that ultimately lead to HUVEC interaction. The evidence is consistent with a mechanism where Phyllofolactones F acts as a modulator of signaling pathways involving CD3 and other compounds that ultimately influence HUVEC function. The strength of the evidence is moderate, as the scores are relatively close, but the exact nature of the interaction remains uncertain due to the complexity of the pathways involved.",
